WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.392
FP32 uses 32-bit floating-point values and has been a common training and reference precision.

00:00:05.392 --> 00:00:07.660
FP16 uses half as many bits,

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:11.063
reducing memory and taking advantage of supported accelerator operations.

00:00:11.063 --> 00:00:14.465
BF16 also uses 16 bits but allocates them differently,

00:00:14.465 --> 00:00:19.757
preserving a broad numerical range that is useful in training and some inference workflows.

00:00:19.757 --> 00:00:23.915
Hardware support determines whether a precision provides an actual speed benefit.

00:00:23.915 --> 00:00:28.073
INT8 represents quantized values with 8-bit integers, often with per-tensor, per-channel,

00:00:28.073 --> 00:00:29.585
or grouped scale information.

00:00:29.585 --> 00:00:32.610
Four-bit and lower-bit weight formats reduce storage further,

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.146
but the nominal bit count does not include every scale, lookup, alignment,

00:00:37.146 --> 00:00:38.280
or metadata cost.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.572
Actual file and loaded sizes are therefore larger than parameters multiplied by bits alone.

00:00:43.572 --> 00:00:45.840
Weights are the learned model parameters.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.620
Activations are intermediate values produced while the model processes input.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.157
A system may store weights at low precision while computing activations or

00:00:54.157 --> 00:00:57.181
selected layers at higher precision to preserve quality.

00:00:57.181 --> 00:01:00.961
The key-value cache has its own format and memory cost,

00:01:00.961 --> 00:01:03.985
which grows with context length and active sequences.

00:01:03.985 --> 00:01:05.875
Precision affects more than size.

00:01:05.875 --> 00:01:08.899
It changes numerical resolution, supported kernels, memory traffic,

00:01:08.899 --> 00:01:10.411
and sometimes output behavior.

00:01:10.411 --> 00:01:15.704
Lower is not automatically faster if the hardware or runtime lacks an efficient implementation.

00:01:15.704 --> 00:01:20.996
Treat labels such as 4-bit as a starting description, then inspect the quantization method,

00:01:20.996 --> 00:01:24.020
runtime support, measured memory, speed, and task quality.
